## Architectural Deep Dive

### 1. Chip Select (CS) - GPIO 10
The `SD_CS` pin functions as a hardware toggle switch. Because several components on this board share the exact same SPI interface buses, the ESP32-S3 uses this line to isolate specific data traffic. Pulling this pin **LOW (0V)** opens the digital gateway for the SD card to send or receive information. Returning it to a **HIGH (3.3V)** state silences the card completely, freeing up the shared data highway so other peripherals can communicate.

### 2. Master Out Slave In (MOSI) - GPIO 11
This is a unidirectional outbound data line flowing from the ESP32-S3 processor straight to the storage device. When the calendar engine finishes downloading an interactive `.ics` update from a server over Wi-Fi, it pushes the parsed stream data down this channel to overwrite or save your `/calendar.ics` file inside the local storage blocks.

### 3. Master In Slave Out (MISO) - GPIO 13
This is a unidirectional inbound data line flowing from the micro SD card chip back to the central ESP32-S3 processor core. Every time you tap the touchscreen navigation buttons (`<` or `>`) to flip calendar pages, the runtime environment reads from this stream to check dates and look for scheduled items without needing to query the internet.

### 4. Serial Clock (SCK) - GPIO 12
The clock pin produces a continuous, strict metronome beat managed by the ESP32-S3. Digital storage processes require microsecond-accurate data synchronization. With every rising or falling edge pulse driven across Pin 12, exactly one binary bit of calendar text is synchronized across the MOSI and MISO paths, preventing corrupted data frames during active operations.
